<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    helm's notification small icon: a terminal prompt, `>_`.

    ## Why it is white on transparent, with nothing inside it

    Since Android 5 the system throws away every colour channel of a small
    icon and keeps only ALPHA, then refills the shape with a tint of its
    own choosing (the manifest's `default_notification_color` for the
    Firebase path, `AndroidNotificationDetails.color` for helm's own).
    Anything opaque becomes one flat colour.

    That is why the launcher icon this replaced rendered as a plain white
    square: every pixel of a photographic icon is opaque, so its silhouette
    is its own bounding box. Detail here is therefore drawn as GAPS — the
    space between the chevron and the underscore is transparent, not dark.
    A "hollow" shape drawn with a dark fill would silhouette solid.

    ## Why this shape

    `>_` is the one glyph that reads as "terminal" at 24dp, and it survives
    the size: two strokes, no enclosed counters, no fine detail to lose. A
    ship's wheel matches the app's NAME better and was rejected on exactly
    that measurement — six spokes and a hub become mush in a 24dp status
    bar, and the gaps that make it legible are the first thing to close up.

    ## The geometry

    Laid out on the 24dp canvas so the glyph's inked extent — endpoints
    plus the 1.5 the round caps add at each end — is symmetric: 3.1 of
    clearance on the left and right, 3.7 top and bottom. Stroke 3 is 12.5%
    of the canvas, which is heavy for an in-app icon and correct for a
    status bar, where the icon may be drawn at 18dp or less.

    Vector rather than PNG deliberately. The usual warning against vector
    notification icons is about VectorDrawableCompat, which the system UI
    process cannot inflate on pre-21 devices; this is a framework
    VectorDrawable and minSdk is 24, so that case cannot arise.
-->
<vector xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    android:width="24dp"
    android:height="24dp"
    android:viewportWidth="24"
    android:viewportHeight="24">

    <!-- The prompt chevron. -->
    <path
        android:fillColor="#00000000"
        android:pathData="M4.6,5.2 L11.3,12 L4.6,18.8"
        android:strokeColor="#FFFFFFFF"
        android:strokeLineCap="round"
        android:strokeLineJoin="round"
        android:strokeWidth="3" />

    <!-- The cursor, sitting on the chevron's baseline. -->
    <path
        android:fillColor="#00000000"
        android:pathData="M13.4,18.8 L19.4,18.8"
        android:strokeColor="#FFFFFFFF"
        android:strokeLineCap="round"
        android:strokeWidth="3" />
</vector>
